  "answer_text": "Here's a breakdown of typical linebacker jersey numbers in the NFL:\n\n### Current NFL Rules (as of 2021 expansion)\n\nLinebackers are currently allowed to wear numbers **1\u201359** and **90\u201399**.\n\n### Most Traditionally Common Numbers\n\n- **50s (50\u201359):** Numbers 50 to 59 are the most historically traditional range for linebackers.\n- **90s (90\u201399):** Numbers 90 to 99 are also commonly worn by linebackers (shared with defensive linemen).\n\n### History of the Number Range\n\n- In **1984**, the NFL allowed linebackers to wear jersey numbers in the **90\u201399** range, since more teams were making use of the 3\u20134 defense and were quickly exhausting numbers for linebackers, who previously were only allowed to wear numbers in the 50\u201359 range.\n- The NFL Competition Committee made another change in **2015**, allowing linebackers to use numbers **40 through 49** for the first time.\n- In **2021**, NFL owners passed a rule expanding available numbers, allowing linebackers to wear **1\u201379 and 90\u201399** \u2014 though the official NFL operations page currently lists it as **1\u201359 and 90\u201399**.\n\n### Quick Summary Table\n\n| Number Range | Notes |\n|---|---|\n| **1\u201359** | Allowed for linebackers |\n| **90\u201399** | Allowed for linebackers (shared with D-linemen) |\n| **50\u201359** | Most *classic/traditional* LB numbers |\n| **40\u201349** | Opened up in 2015 |\n\nSo while a linebacker can technically wear a wide variety of numbers today, you'll most commonly see them in the **50s** and **90s** in practice.",
